  "translationRepairs": [
    ["s0005","idiomatic","In Kangxi 8 the Kangxi emperor, on Verbiest's advice, commissioned six new instruments: ecliptic theodolite, equatorial theodolite, azimuth theodolite, altitude quadrant, astronomical quadrant, and celestial globe.","In Kangxi 8 the Kangxi emperor, on Verbiest's advice, commissioned six new instruments: ecliptic theodolite, equatorial theodolite, azimuth theodolite, altitude quadrant, sextant, and celestial globe.","s0005 lists 紀限儀 among the six remade instruments; s0063 states its arc is 全圓六分之一 / 六十度, i.e. a sextant. Rendering it as 'astronomical quadrant' contradicts that geometry; 'sextant' is accurate.","high","applied"],
    ["s0011","literal","The half ring borne above, front and rear running true north-south, rises straight to the celestial pole and cuts the horizon at center.","The half ring borne above, front and rear running true north-south, rises straight to the zenith and cuts the horizon at center.","s0011 has 上直天頂; 天頂 is the zenith, not the celestial pole (北極). The prior literal misreads it; 'rises straight to the zenith' matches the Chinese and the idiomatic.","high","applied"],
    ["s0050","literal","The line within the axle is always the plumb line to the celestial pole.","The line within the axle is always the plumb line to the zenith.","s0050 says 恆為天頂之垂線; 天頂 means zenith. 'Plumb line to the celestial pole' is wrong; 'plumb line to the zenith' restores the sense already correct in the idiomatic.","high","applied"],
    ["s0063","idiomatic","The astronomical quadrant's arc is one sixth of a circle, graduated to sixty degrees.","The sextant's arc is one sixth of a circle, graduated to sixty degrees.","s0063 itself defines 紀限儀 as an arc of one sixth of a full circle divided into sixty degrees. Calling it an 'astronomical quadrant' contradicts that definition; 'sextant' is the correct English term.","high","applied"],
    ["s0086","literal","A single ring leans obliquely as the fixed ecliptic equator circle; both faces are engraved with the circuit of the sun's twelve hours; zi and wu noon rightly occupy the meridian double ring's hollow half, knotting at its midpoint—that is celestial latitude.","A single ring leans obliquely as the fixed celestial equator circle; both faces are engraved with the circuit of the sun's twelve hours; zi and wu noon rightly occupy the meridian double ring's hollow half, knotting at its midpoint—that is celestial latitude.","s0086 names the ring 天常赤道圈, the fixed celestial equator, not an ecliptic equator. 'Fixed ecliptic equator circle' invents an ecliptic sense absent from the Chinese; 'fixed celestial equator circle' matches 天常赤道圈.","high","applied"],
    ["s0091","literal","Again according to the capital's north polar altitude in degrees and minutes, count upward to complete one quadrant—that is the celestial pole.","Again according to the capital's north polar altitude in degrees and minutes, count upward to complete one quadrant—that is the zenith.","s0091 ends 即天頂 after counting one quadrant up from the capital's north polar altitude. 天頂 is the zenith, not the celestial pole; the proposed replacement matches the Chinese and the idiomatic.","high","applied"],
    ["s0093","literal","At the celestial pole set a small nail to suspend the plumb line; the plumb rightly reaches the earth's center and also rightly cuts the double ring's face.","At the zenith set a small nail to suspend the plumb line; the plumb rightly reaches the earth's center and also rightly cuts the double ring's face.","s0093 places the nail 于天頂 to hang the plumb toward 地心. 天頂 is the zenith; suspending from the celestial pole would misstate both the Chinese and the geometry of a plumb.","high","applied"]
  ],
